+## Subtopics
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The error computation is linear with respect to the number of clusters $K$ and the number of training views $R$, and proportional to the number of pixels in the visibility masks. Gradient computation requires differentiating the rendering process with respect to weights, which depends on the specific renderer's differentiability.
-*   **Strengths:** It provides specific feedback for individual voxel clusters, enabling the optimization of anisotropic scattering parameters that vary spatially. It handles parameter heterogeneity better than global MSE.
-*   **Limitations:** It requires pre-computed cluster assignments and visibility masks. It is sensitive to the quality of the clustering step.
-*   **When to use:** Use when optimizing spatially varying anisotropic scattering parameters in volume rendering, particularly when global intensity metrics fail to guide the optimization.
-*   **When not to use:** Do not use for global color correction or when scattering parameters are assumed to be uniform across the entire volume.
+- **Time Complexity:** The error calculation is $O(K \cdot R \cdot N)$, where $R$ is the number of views and $N$ is the number of pixels in the visibility sets. Gradient estimation via path tracing is the dominant cost, scaling with the number of samples per pixel and the complexity of the scattering model [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the cluster assignments for all voxels and the visibility masks for each cluster and view. The weights array is $O(K \cdot m)$ [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Effectively handles parameter heterogeneity by attributing errors to specific spatial clusters. It avoids the ambiguity of global mean metrics, leading to more stable convergence in optimizing anisotropic scattering parameters [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computed voxel clustering and visibility masks. Performance depends on the quality of the clustering; poor clustering can lead to suboptimal weight sharing [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Restricted Mean Error Metric was introduced in the context of downsampling scattering parameters for rendering anisotropic media. It addresses limitations in previous work (e.g., Zhao et al. 2011; Khungurn et al. 2015) that used global mean intensity, which failed to identify specific voxel clusters needing correction. The method is detailed in the paper "Downsampling scattering parameters for rendering anisotropic media" published in the SIGGRAPH Asia 2016 Proceedings [S1].
+The Restricted Mean Error Metric was introduced in the context of downsampling scattering parameters for rendering anisotropic media. It addresses limitations in previous work by Zhao et al. (2011) and Khungurn et al. (2015), which used global mean pixel intensity for error metrics. The global mean approach failed to provide sufficient information for correcting specific voxel clusters, leading to local minima in optimization. The restricted mean approach was developed to enable efficient optimization of SGGX lobe weights by clustering voxels and sharing weight factors within clusters [S1].
 
